1. What you may do

  a. Use it. You may get the Software from its official sources (section 3), build
     it, install it and use it on as many computers as you like, for any purpose. This
     includes your own commercial work: you may use uehub to manage the assets and
     engines of a game or project that you sell.

  b. Read and study it. You may read the source code and learn from it.

  c. Change it to contribute. You may fork the official repository on GitHub and
     change your fork in order to propose those changes to the Author, through pull
     requests, issues or patches. You may build and run your changed version on your
     own computers to develop and test it.


2. What you may not do

  a. Redistribute it. You may not give, publish or share copies of the Software or of
     a changed version, as source code or as builds: no mirrors, downloads, packages
     (in the AUR, Flathub, Snap, a distribution's repositories or anywhere else),
     installers, or bundles with other software. People get uehub from its official
     sources. A fork on GitHub made as allowed in 1.c is not redistribution, as long
     as it keeps this license and you do not publish builds or packages made from it.

  b. Make money from it. You may not sell the Software or a changed version, charge
     for access to it, for downloading it, for installing it or for any of its
     features, show ads in it, include it in a paid product or service, or earn money
     from the Software itself in any other way. uehub is free and stays free. This
     does not limit what you make with it (see 1.a).

  c. Pass it off as yours. You may not remove or change the copyright notices, this
     license or the credits to the Author; present the Software, a changed version or
     its code as your own work; or use the name "uehub" or its logo for anything other
     than the Software itself.

  d. Take its code elsewhere. You may not copy the Software's code, in whole or in
     substantial part, into another project.


3. Official sources

The official sources of the Software are its GitHub repository, the releases published
there, and the packages the Author publishes, such as the uehub package in the AUR
maintained by the Author.


